WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.396
Leadership communication should explain PQC risk without hype.

00:00:03.396 --> 00:00:08.341
The message is not that every encrypted record is suddenly exposed or

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:11.225
that a single date will change everything.

00:00:11.225 --> 00:00:16.169
The better message is that some widely used public-key assumptions may not

00:00:16.169 --> 00:00:20.290
remain adequate in the presence of cryptographically relevant quantum computers,

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:25.234
and that migration has enough dependency work that responsible organizations should prepare

00:00:25.234 --> 00:00:27.294
before urgency forces rushed decisions.

00:00:27.294 --> 00:00:29.766
Governance turns that message into action.

00:00:29.766 --> 00:00:34.299
Policies may need to describe approved algorithms, library expectations, certificate practices,

00:00:34.299 --> 00:00:37.183
data retention considerations, vendor requirements, exception handling,

00:00:37.183 --> 00:00:38.419
and documentation standards.

00:00:38.419 --> 00:00:42.539
Procurement teams may need questions for cloud providers, appliance vendors,

00:00:42.539 --> 00:00:45.836
identity platforms, managed service providers, and software suppliers.

00:00:45.836 --> 00:00:49.544
Risk registers should track systems with long-lived confidential data,

00:00:49.544 --> 00:00:52.840
unsupported dependencies, migration blockers, and accepted residual risk.

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:56.136
Executives usually do not need deep cryptographic theory.

00:00:56.136 --> 00:01:00.257
They need practical timelines, inventory progress, decisions required, funding needs,

00:01:00.257 --> 00:01:03.553
dependency risks, vendor readiness, and consequences of delay.

00:01:03.553 --> 00:01:08.909
A useful dashboard might show what percentage of exposed services have been inventoried,

00:01:08.909 --> 00:01:12.206
which high-value systems depend on quantum-vulnerable public-key mechanisms,

00:01:12.206 --> 00:01:17.150
which vendors have committed support, and which pilots have produced usable results.

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:22.919
Training matters because many teams touch cryptography without thinking of themselves as cryptography teams.

00:01:22.919 --> 00:01:24.155
Developers choose libraries.

00:01:24.155 --> 00:01:25.803
Platform teams manage certificates.

00:01:25.803 --> 00:01:27.451
Operations teams troubleshoot handshakes.

00:01:27.451 --> 00:01:30.335
Procurement teams ask vendors about roadmap support.

00:01:30.335 --> 00:01:31.983
Security teams assess risk.

00:01:31.983 --> 00:01:36.104
Good governance gives each group enough shared language to coordinate.

00:01:36.104 --> 00:01:41.460
That shared language is what prevents PQC from becoming either panic or paperwork.
